## The CPU-queue counters, and their limit on this bench
`show platform counter sdma` exposes Marvell SDMA receive-queue counters.
  rxDmaPcktCnt[0] -- a plain cumulative counter. copy-to-cpu lands here, and it is CLEAN:
                     it sits at 0 on this bench and moves only when a copy-to-cpu rule matches.
  rxDmaPcktCnt[6] -- CLEAR-ON-READ, and it carries the background control traffic this bench
                     generates continuously (RSTP BPDUs, LLDP, and loop-protection detect
                     frames at one per second on every port). Measured idle rate: 40 counts in
                     8 s. Ten test packets against that floor is not separable, so queue 6 is
                     NOT used as evidence in these cases. Recorded so nobody reads its absence
                     as a negative result.

## Precondition carried from the case
T44989's own precondition cites **CR30477** for STP interference, and the ACL guide warns that
send-to-cpu lands traffic on a CPU queue that "could result in EPSR healthcheck messages and
other control packets being dropped". RSTP is running on all three units, so
`show spanning-tree brief` is captured before and after every send-to-cpu step.
`access-list hardware-deny-log` was NOT enabled at any point -- the guide warns that matching
packets are dropped if it is on while a copy-to-cpu or send-to-cpu ACL exists.

## Steps and evidence

### STEP 0 -- STP baseline before the send-to-cpu rule
x960-56YSQ#show spanning-tree brief
  Default: Root Id 8000:0000cd423029
  Default: Bridge Id 8000:e01aead472cb
  sa1             8000:0000cd423029    8195       Rootport         Forwarding
  port1.0.10      8000:e01aead472cb    8392       Designated       Forwarding
  port1.0.48      8000:e01aead472cb    83b8       Designated       Forwarding

### STEP 1 -- apply send-to-cpu and prove BOTH halves of its definition
The ACL guide defines send-to-cpu as "to the CPU and NOT forwarded".
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.48 ; no access-group t44991
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list hardware t44989
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#send-to-cpu ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.48 ; access-group t44989
x960-56YSQ#show access-list t44989
  Hardware IP access list t44989
      4 send-to-cpu ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32

  HALF ONE -- the rule matched:
x960-56YSQ#show access-list counters t44989
      Packet Hits: 8                        <-- all 8 test packets matched

  HALF TWO -- NOT forwarded:
host# ping -c 8 -i 0.3 -I 10.38.22.65 10.38.22.70
  100% packet loss                          <-- the far side never received them
host# ping -c 3 -I 10.38.22.66 10.38.22.70
  0% packet loss                            <-- control: a source no rule matches still passes

x960-56YSQ#show platform counter sdma       (before, then after)
              0 : sdmaRegs.rxDmaPcktCnt[0]  ->      0 : sdmaRegs.rxDmaPcktCnt[0]
  CPU queue 0 did NOT move -- send-to-cpu does not use the queue copy-to-cpu uses.
  => STEP 1 PASS: matching frames were consumed by the switch and never reached the far side,
     while an identical flow from a non-matching source was unaffected. The rule's own counter
     proves the frames were matched rather than lost, so "100% packet loss" is the ACL acting,
     not a broken path.

### STEP 2 -- STP undisturbed: CR30477 did NOT reproduce
x960-56YSQ#show spanning-tree brief
  Default: Root Id 8000:0000cd423029        <-- unchanged
  Default: Bridge Id 8000:e01aead472cb      <-- unchanged
  sa1             8000:0000cd423029    8195       Rootport         Forwarding
  port1.0.10      8000:e01aead472cb    8392       Designated       Forwarding
  port1.0.48      8000:e01aead472cb    83b8       Designated       Forwarding
  => STEP 2 PASS: root bridge, bridge id, root port and every forwarding port are identical to
     the STEP 0 baseline across the send-to-cpu step. The STP interference CR30477 describes
     did not reproduce on this build.

### STEP 3 -- the copy-to-cpu / send-to-cpu contrast
Run back to back with T44991 on the identical setup, binding point and traffic:

  action        ACL hits   CPU queue 0    forwarded to the far side?
  copy-to-cpu       8        0 -> 8       YES  (0% packet loss)
  send-to-cpu       8        0 -> 0       NO   (100% packet loss)

  => STEP 3 PASS: both actions matched the same 8 packets, so the only difference is what the
     switch did with them -- forwarded as well as copied, versus consumed. That is exactly the
     documented distinction, and neither result could be produced by the other action.

VERDICT: PASS -- a named hardware ACL with the send-to-cpu action, bound to a port, consumes
matching frames: its counter records all 8 as matched while the far side receives none (100%
packet loss), and a non-matching source on the same port is unaffected. CPU receive-queue 0,
which copy-to-cpu advances, does not move. Spanning tree was byte-for-byte unchanged across
the step, so the CR30477 interference cited in the case's precondition did not reproduce.
